   "content": [
      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htChartMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p><p style=\"font-style: italic;\" id=\"htChinaZhongguo\"></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nFor a better understanding of the content in this text, we recommend studying Volume 1 of the first steps section, where numbers, an introduction to limits, and fundamental operations are taught (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team (2024). First steps",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we want to build something, several steps are necessary. For example, we need to plan, present the project to the people who will help us build, take measurements, and finally carry out the construction.\n\nAll these steps have something in common. Since we are building within nature, using natural materials that undergo transformations, we will need to use the language of nature — mathematics.\n\nGiven this scenario, let us make an initial reflection.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: About language</b><br /><br />So far, we have seen how to use numbers.<br /><br />But what do these numbers we have seen so far tell us about the constructions we see around the planet?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_andes",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### An indigenous example\n\nWhen we observe Machu Picchu (**Figure 1**), built by the Incas (<htcite0>), we might ask ourselves: how did they manage to build something so monumental?",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4149b25a-3a8f-4bb2-b663-4dab5a5d885b",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Incas (Tawantinsuyu)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"mp\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('mp', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Photo of the city of Machu Picchu, Peru. Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the city.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2017",
                     "month": "8",
                     "day": "16"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "However, the answers could lie in the details that are ignored when we are impressed by the city, or in the lack of knowledge about indigenous culture.\n\n#### Looking around\n\nObserve behind the city of Machu Picchu and the region where it was built.\n\nAs you will notice, the city is in a natural region surrounded by many mountains.\n\nIf we take a piece of the mountain and start breaking it, we will see that it is formed by smaller parts that were naturally stacked (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2fd2e872-c5d8-4f1b-91dc-97df40597c4e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Evolução do conhecimento (Átomo)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: An inspiration</b><br /><br />How could we confirm that the Incas and other peoples of the Andes observed nature to carry out their constructions?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A cultural response\n\nWhen we study how indigenous peoples have been representing numbers in the American continent (*Abya Yala*), we can observe that, since the emergence of the quipus shown in **Figure 2**, possibly thousands of years ago (<htcite0>; <htcite1>), numbers have always been represented vertically, like constructions.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c2ff41fc-7833-4dc2-8882-eaed12a065b7",
                     "text": "Mann, Charles C.",
                     "page": "pp. 1008",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fddfb494-8fb6-4dee-bf73-ba3bb0fc88fd",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Copiando a Natureza",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"qp\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('qp', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 2</b>: Panel at the archaeological site of Caral, © ZonaArqueólogicaCaral, (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2017",
                     "month": "5",
                     "day": "31"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Furthermore, in other *History Tracers* texts we learn that millennia later, the Incas developed a calculator, the Yupana, where they also wrote their numbers vertically (<htcite0>).\n\nIt is not only in tools that we observe this step-by-step construction. Dhavit Prem, developer of the *Yupana Inka Tawa Pukllay* method, when explaining how numbers are positioned on the *Yupana*, informs us that in ancient Aymara, when we say 6 in the Aymara language we would say *1 after 5* — exactly the way we represent it on the Yupana (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6fd2997b-caf1-4620-a58f-1140388ab8c1",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Mudança de direção (Parte II)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d24a01d9-2d63-40aa-9573-fb6662f6d0bb",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit (2024). Yupana Inka Tawa Pukllay (YITP): Redescubriendo la matemática Inka 500 años después. Dhavit Prem Channel",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/imy9iMc4eGo?si=VHQk75m_HtCJMjMw&amp;start=2454\" title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe><br /><b>Video 1</b>: Rediscovering Inca mathematics 500 years later.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "4",
                     "day": "26"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: A human pattern</b><br /><br />Besides the indigenous peoples of America (<i>Abya Yala</i>), could other populations have also built by observing nature and using this knowledge?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_asia",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### An Asian perspective\n\nWhen we look at China (Zhongguo 中國), we can also find monumental constructions such as the Great Wall (**Figure 3**), which is also surrounded by mountains:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img class=\"imgcenter\"  id=\"imgCW\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCW', '0%')\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: Great Wall of China. Photo taken by Giovanni Boccardi and provided on the UNESCO website (<htcite0>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/licenses/6\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 IGO</a> license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "674939ef-1f42-4c1d-a614-17bd22aba67e",
                     "text": "UNESCO",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Just like the indigenous peoples of America (*Abya Yala*), languages such as Chinese, Japanese, and Korean also help us understand and plan constructions. For example, numbers like 11, 12, and 13 are spoken as *ten-one, ten-two, ten-three* (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "31070f9b-d4f7-46ed-8ca3-88dd8dee793c",
                     "text": "Nyota, S., & Mapara, J.",
                     "page": "pp. 195",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2008",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_origin",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### From Africa to the world\n\nHumanity originated in the African continent, as various studies have shown in recent centuries (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>), and then spread throughout the world.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2be849f1-551b-44eb-8fc6-87027dbf8a68",
                     "text": "Mounier, A., & Mirazón Lahr, M.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2019",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "064c3691-1f4c-491b-b643-150c011b4525",
                     "text": "Goebel, Ted, et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2008",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cff99ec5-cb25-40ef-b2ee-410a75e73a6e",
                     "text": "National Geographic Education",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "e6ffacf6-f9ac-46ea-b046-3f6ad2c9a744",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team (2024). Human migration",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgGeo24\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgGeo24', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 4</b>: In this map, the continent is highlighted © Mapswire (<htcite0>)</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "dd4f3c8a-6624-4df5-93ac-37e0a4be9fdc",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As our knowledge develops from earlier knowledge, and as we saw in the work of Dr. Francisco González A. Redondo, in Africa we have one of the earliest counting systems based on baboon bones (<htcite0>), we can also find among African peoples environments and, consequently, languages that may help people create construction systems.\n\nAnd in fact, this can be verified through one of the Bantu peoples, the Shona people, who built the Great Zimbabwe (**Figure 5**), which also has names for numbers with a constructive logic in its original language (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a4f22e25-5839-407f-a1aa-634521d52b42",
                     "text": "González Redondo, Francisco A., et al. (2010). Prehistoria de La Matemática y Mente Moderna: Pensamiento Matemático y Recursividad En El Paleolítico Franco-Cantábrico. <i>Dynamis, vol. 30</i>. 2010. DOI.org (Crossref),",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "31070f9b-d4f7-46ed-8ca3-88dd8dee793c",
                     "text": "Nyota, S., & Mapara, J.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2008",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img class=\"imgcenter\" id=\"imgWALL\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgWALL', '0%')\"/><b>Figure 5</b>: Great Zimbabwe National Monument (<htcite0>). Photo taken by Erik Eschweiler-Dienerowitz and provided on the UNESCO website (<htcite1>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/licenses/6\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 IGO</a> license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19934031-f0f5-4a1d-83c3-6391b5acaff8",
                     "text": "The National Museum and Monuments of Zimbabwe",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b7d68d4b-67a9-4624-9ce6-9b0e2f01c50e",
                     "text": "UNESCO",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "21"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "These facts related to Shona culture have led Dr. Wilbert to develop research associated with the knowledge developed by his culture in the areas of mathematics (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "cc162709-bdc3-469b-a208-e900f4570842",
                     "text": "Sadomba, Zvakanyorwa Wilbert",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nSince there are no races in our species (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2>), we can understand that the way we build and reach conclusions is similar across all peoples.\n\nWith that in mind, let us make one final reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2659babf-36dc-480f-a3d9-d37b8c4e1159",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "29"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "723b0735-b8c4-4b02-8ce6-6da033ca2454",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "NIH",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "29"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f20eb3b0-891f-4acf-855f-e9b4200a2b57",
                     "text": "Brewer, R.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2006",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "29"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 4: Inherited or converged?</b><br /><br />The knowledge developed by all peoples through observations, which ended up being reflected in their languages, was it inherited from other peoples or did it arise independently in each culture?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we look at nature around the constructions in the figures we provided in the text, it is evident that people could observe what surrounds them and try to recreate something by repeating nature's patterns.\n\nHowever, when we talk about language, the scenario is more complex. To give you an idea, the Indo-European languages possibly emerged after the first humans arrived in America (*Abya Yala*) (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>). With that, the names for numbers very likely have different origins, even though peoples arrived at similar ideas.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "92105889-8c32-4b59-93c8-79e43bd3e96f",
                     "text": "Starikovskaya, et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "01",
                        "day": "07"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "51615cff-ff86-4efe-8e43-07314f1db742",
                     "text": "Gusareva, E. S., et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1070f062-f6c1-4cc5-8732-83b31c8e5137",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team (2025). Indigenous Abya Yala (America)",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "77c50e46-78a9-46c0-adb3-55d529a4d749",
                     "text": "Bouckaert, R., Lemey, P., Dunn, M., Greenhill, S. J., Alekseyenko, A. V., Drummond, A. J., Gray, R. D., Suchard, M. A., & Atkinson, Q. D. (2012). Mapping the Origins and Expansion of the Indo-European Language Family. Science, 337(6097), 957–960. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219669",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
